1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,512
Chapter 68: Sexually Transmitted Infections.

3
00:00:06,512 --> 00:00:35,387
Syphilis Gonorrhea Chlamydial Infection Herpes Simplex Virus Human Papillomavirus Vaginitis Human Immunodeficiency Virus References Sexually transmitted infections (STIs) include syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydial and trichomonal infections, and infections caused by human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), herpes simplex virus (HSV), human papillomavirus (HPV), and hepatitis B and C viruses.

4
00:00:35,387 --> 00:00:41,562
In describing their pathogenesis, horizontal transmission is transfer to a contemporary.

5
00:00:41,562 --> 00:00:51,912
Instead, vertical transmission refers to passage of an infectious agent from the mother to her fetus through the placenta, during labor or delivery, or by breastfeeding.

6
00:00:51,912 --> 00:00:55,312
Many STIs can harm the mother or fetus.

7
00:00:55,312 --> 00:01:02,187
Thus, these are sought through both asymptomatic screening and symptomatic diagnostic testing and then treated.

8
00:01:02,187 --> 00:01:10,587
For this, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) provides guidelines, which are described throughout this chapter.

9
00:01:10,587 --> 00:01:20,662
For STI screening and diagnosis, serological testing is recommended in some cases and identifies antibodies formed by the patient against the pathogen.

10
00:01:20,662 --> 00:01:31,262
In others, direct detection of an infectious agent by culture or by a nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) of body fluids or lesions is suitable.

11
00:01:31,262 --> 00:01:37,987
NAATs include any molecular method used to detect the presence of DNA or RNA of a pathogen.

12
00:01:37,987 --> 00:01:48,312
NAATs may be polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests (most common), transcription-mediated amplification (TMA) tests, or others.

13
00:01:48,312 --> 00:01:55,712
NAATs may be qualitative (detecting presence or absence only) or quantitative depending on the specific test.

14
00:01:55,712 --> 00:02:02,187
The terms NAAT and PCR may be used interchangeably in some cases when appropriate.

15
00:02:02,187 --> 00:02:10,725
If one STI is identified, patients are offered screening for all STIs, because these infections are commonly comorbid.

16
00:02:10,725 --> 00:02:17,750
Treatment of most STIs is associated with improved pregnancy outcome and prevention of perinatal morbidity.

17
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:24,762
Logically, education, screening, treatment, and prevention are essential components of prenatal care.

18
00:02:24,762 --> 00:02:26,475
Syphilis.

19
00:02:26,475 --> 00:02:31,100
This infection remains a major threat for both mother and fetus.

20
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:43,250
Since 2001, the combined rate of primary and secondary syphilis has risen almost yearly, and among women in 2019, the rate was 4 cases per 100,000 persons .

21
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:50,550
For congenital syphilis, the rate in 2019 was 48 cases per 100,000 live births.

22
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:55,150
This is a nearly 300 percent rise since 2015.

23
00:02:55,150 --> 00:03:06,337
Of risks, higher congenital syphilis rates are linked to inadequate prenatal care, substance use during pregnancy, African American race, and lack of screening and treatment .

24
00:03:06,337 --> 00:03:13,725
The CDC also includes sex work, multiple or new sexual partners, incarceration, or homelessness .

25
00:03:13,725 --> 00:03:17,550
Syphilis remains a significant global health problem .

26
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:20,100
# Pathogenesis.

27
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:24,975
Syphilis is caused by the spirochetal bacterium Treponema pallidum.

28
00:03:24,975 --> 00:03:35,162
With horizontal transmission, minute abrasions on the vaginal mucosa provide entry, and cervical eversion, hyperemia, and friability raise this risk.

29
00:03:35,162 --> 00:03:41,550
Spirochetes replicate and disseminate through lymphatic channels and then hematogenously if untreated.

30
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:46,525
During vertical transmission, spirochetes readily cross the placenta.

31
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:56,087
This is the most common route, but neonatal infection may result from contact with spirochetes from lesions at delivery or across the placental membranes.

32
00:03:56,087 --> 00:04:09,437
Fetal infection develops in >50 percent of untreated early maternal syphilis cases and in up to 10 percent of late latent disease (Fiumara, 1975; Hollier, 2001).

33
00:04:09,437 --> 00:04:12,525
# Clinical Manifestations.

34
00:04:12,525 --> 00:04:18,800
Maternal Syphilis and Definitions This is staged according to clinical features and disease duration.

35
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:25,462
Primary syphilis is diagnosed by its characteristic chancre, which develops at the inoculation site.

36
00:04:25,462 --> 00:04:34,175
Typically, a solitary, painless lesion has a raised, firm border and a red, smooth ulcerated base without significant pus .

37
00:04:34,175 --> 00:04:37,787
Nonsuppurative lymphadenopathy may develop.

38
00:04:37,787 --> 00:04:43,487
A chancre usually resolves spontaneously in 2 to 8 weeks, even if untreated.

39
00:04:43,487 --> 00:04:49,250
Multiple lesions, if found, are predominantly in women with HIV co-infection.

40
00:04:49,250 --> 00:04:51,775
Figure 68-1.

41
00:04:51,775 --> 00:04:53,900
Primary syphilis.

42
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:58,287
Chancres show a raised, firm border and smooth base and may

43
00:04:58,444 --> 00:05:01,731
be singular or multiple, as seen here.

44
00:05:01,681 --> 00:05:10,244
Arrows point to two reciprocal or “kissing” lesions, which develop as infection on one side spreads to the other when tissues are apposed.

45
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:20,344
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Allison J. Tracy, MD.) • Secondary syphilis stems from spirochete dissemination to multiple organ systems.

46
00:05:20,344 --> 00:05:28,919
Manifestations develop 4 to 10 weeks after the chancre appears and include dermatological abnormalities in up to 90 percent.

47
00:05:28,919 --> 00:05:37,031
A diffuse macular rash, plantar and palmar circular lesions, patchy alopecia, and mucous patches may be seen .

48
00:05:37,031 --> 00:05:42,794
Condylomata lata are fleshcolored flat nodules found on the perineum and perianus.

49
00:05:42,794 --> 00:05:47,319
Mucosal lesions teem with spirochetes and are highly infectious.

50
00:05:47,319 --> 00:05:52,894
Women may have constitutional symptoms such as fever, headache, and myalgias.

51
00:05:52,894 --> 00:06:01,031
Hepatitis, nephropathy, ocular changes, anterior uveitis, and periostitis are less common findings.

52
00:06:01,031 --> 00:06:10,556
Manifestations of secondary syphilis resolve without treatment after 1 to 6 months, despite incomplete clearance of organisms by the immune system.

53
00:06:10,556 --> 00:06:13,031
Figure 68-2.

54
00:06:13,031 --> 00:06:15,281
Secondary syphilis.

55
00:06:15,281 --> 00:06:17,506
A. Condyloma lata.

56
00:06:17,506 --> 00:06:21,169
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Ralynn Brann.

57
00:06:21,169 --> 00:06:23,956
B. Target lesions on the palms.

58
00:06:23,956 --> 00:06:27,106
C. Diffuse maculopapular rash.

59
00:06:27,106 --> 00:06:39,281
With latent syphilis, clinical manifestations of primary or secondary syphilis either have resolved or were never recognized, but infection persists and is identified by serological testing.

60
00:06:39,281 --> 00:06:44,919
Early latent syphilis is subclinical disease acquired within the preceding 12 months.

61
00:06:44,919 --> 00:07:02,781
Timing of infection is determined by laboratory seroconversion results, clearly described symptoms consistent with primary or secondary syphilis within the prior 12 months, or exposure to a sexual partner in the previous 12 months with primary, secondary, or early latent syphilis.

62
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:11,269
If the duration of infection is either unclear or beyond 12 months, the term is latent syphilis, unknown duration or late.

63
00:07:11,269 --> 00:07:19,056
Tertiary syphilis is a slowly progressive disease that affects any organ system but is rarely seen in reproductive-aged women.

64
00:07:19,056 --> 00:07:24,869
Early-stage syphilis includes primary, secondary, and early latent syphilis.

65
00:07:24,869 --> 00:07:36,206
These are associated with high spirochete loads, and partner transmission rates range from 30 to 60 percent (Garnett, 1997; Singh, 1999).

66
00:07:36,206 --> 00:07:42,719
Late-stage syphilis includes latent syphilis, unknown duration or late and tertiary syphilis.

67
00:07:42,719 --> 00:07:48,031
In these, transmission rates decline because of smaller inoculum sizes.

68
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:50,756
# Congenital Syphilis.

69
00:07:50,756 --> 00:07:58,019
Without screening and treatment, approximately 70 percent of infected women will have an adverse pregnancy outcome .

70
00:07:58,019 --> 00:08:05,969
Maternal infection can lead to congenital infection, preterm labor, low birthweight, and fetal or neonatal death .

71
00:08:05,969 --> 00:08:17,819
Because of its immune incompetence prior to midpregnancy, the fetus is less likely to manifest the immunological inflammatory response characteristic of congenital infection before this time .

72
00:08:17,819 --> 00:08:24,394
However, congenital infection is still presumed in all cases, and thus treatment is imperative.

73
00:08:24,394 --> 00:08:30,619
When vertical transmission occurs, severe congenital syphilis progresses along a continuum.

74
00:08:30,619 --> 00:08:37,394
Hepatic abnormalities are followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and then ascites and hydrops.

75
00:08:37,394 --> 00:08:40,669
Stillbirth remains a major complication .

76
00:08:40,669 --> 00:08:51,969
The newborn may have jaundice with petechiae or purpuric skin lesions, lymphadenopathy, rhinitis, pneumonia, myocarditis, nephrosis, or long-bone involvement .

77
00:08:51,969 --> 00:08:54,531
Figure 68-3.

78
00:08:54,531 --> 00:08:56,744
Congenital syphilis.

79
00:08:56,744 --> 00:09:02,044
A. A desquamating skin rash known as pemphigus syphiliticus may be seen.

80
00:09:02,044 --> 00:09:08,069
B. Neonatal lower-extremity long-bone radiographs show a “moth-eaten” appearance (arrows).

81
00:09:08,069 --> 00:09:13,119
C. Enlarged hydropic placenta of a syphilis-infected pregnancy.

82
00:09:13,119 --> 00:09:17,656
With syphilitic infection, the placenta becomes large and pale.

83
00:09:17,656 --> 00:09:28,581
Microscopically, villi lose their characteristic arborization, and blood vessels diminish or are obliterated as a result of endarteritis and stromal cell proliferation.

84
00:09:28,581 --> 00:09:38,094
Sheffield and colleagues (2002c) described these villi in more than 60 percent of placentas from women with untreated syphilis at delivery.

85
00:09:38,094 --> 00:09:46,456
Examination of the umbilical cord may reveal necrotizing funisitis, and spirochetes may be microscopically seen after

86
00:09:46,612 --> 00:09:49,287
immunohistochemical staining .

87
00:09:49,237 --> 00:09:51,637
# Diagnosis.

88
00:09:51,637 --> 00:10:03,887
In their 2018 guidance, the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommends screening for syphilis in all pregnant women to prevent congenital infection.

89
00:10:03,887 --> 00:10:07,449
Testing is performed at the first prenatal visit.

90
00:10:07,449 --> 00:10:18,837
In populations with a high prevalence of syphilis or in persons with the risk factors noted earlier, serological testing is repeated at 28 weeks’ gestation and again at delivery .

91
00:10:18,837 --> 00:10:23,299
Treponema pallidum cannot be cultured from clinical specimens.

92
00:10:23,299 --> 00:10:35,537
However, direct diagnosis of early-stage disease can be completed by darkfield microscopic examination, NAAT, or immunostaining of lesion exudate, tissue, or body fluid.

93
00:10:35,537 --> 00:10:40,762
These methods are not widely available and are less sensitive for blood specimens .

94
00:10:40,762 --> 00:10:46,937
In practice, a diagnosis is made with serological testing coupled with clinical findings.

95
00:10:46,937 --> 00:10:55,637
Two types of serological tests are employed to diagnose and screen, and sequential use of both reduces false-positive diagnoses.

96
00:10:55,637 --> 00:10:59,862
If the first is positive, the second type is then performed.

97
00:10:59,862 --> 00:11:05,737
This combination identifies infection but by itself does not determine disease stage.

98
00:11:05,737 --> 00:11:17,837
Instead, disease stage is assigned based on initial serological titer levels, patient history of potential exposures and prior treatment, and clinical-stage definitions, described earlier.

99
00:11:17,837 --> 00:11:28,974
Traditionally, the first type is nontreponemal testing, and either the Venereal Disease Research Laboratory (VDRL) or the rapid plasma reagin (RPR) is selected.

100
00:11:28,974 --> 00:11:41,562
Both tests measure patient immunoglobulin M and G (IgM and IgG) antibodies formed against cardiolipin that is released from damaged host cells and possibly also from treponemes.

101
00:11:41,562 --> 00:11:51,974
Notably, these same antibodies can also be produced in response to other acute events that include recent vaccination, febrile illness, and pregnancy itself.

102
00:11:51,974 --> 00:12:02,362
They can be produced in response to chronic conditions such as intravenous (IV) drug abuse, systemic lupus erythematosus, aging, leprosy, or cancer.

103
00:12:02,362 --> 00:12:07,537
As such, these all serve as potential sources of false-positive results .

104
00:12:07,537 --> 00:12:18,712
Conversely, early infection may be characterized by very high levels of antibody or antigen, which prevent immune complex formation and lead to false-negative test results.

105
00:12:18,712 --> 00:12:21,087
This is the prozone effect.

106
00:12:21,087 --> 00:12:27,962
Alternatively, seroconversion occurs at around the third week of infection but can take up to 6 weeks .

107
00:12:27,962 --> 00:12:35,212
Thus, women with very early primary syphilis can have initially false-negative serological test results.

108
00:12:35,212 --> 00:12:41,624
With positive nontreponemal test results, findings are quantified and expressed as titers.

109
00:12:41,624 --> 00:12:50,587
Because titers reflect disease activity, they rise during early syphilis and often exceed levels of 1:32 in secondary syphilis.

110
00:12:50,587 --> 00:12:55,724
A nontreponemal titer is usually measured on the day of treatment initiation.

111
00:12:55,724 --> 00:13:00,987
Subsequent titer declines are considered relative to this day-of-treatment titer.

112
00:13:00,987 --> 00:13:08,024
The second type of serological test is used to confirm the presence of T pallidum–specific antibodies.

113
00:13:08,024 --> 00:13:20,174
These tests include fluorescent treponemalantibody absorption tests (FTA-ABS), T pallidum passive particle agglutination (TP-PA) tests, and various immunoassays .

114
00:13:20,174 --> 00:13:27,112
Of note, these treponemal-specific tests generally remain positive throughout life, even after treatment.

115
00:13:27,112 --> 00:13:34,762
Antibodies may be detected by treponemal assays up to a few weeks earlier than those detected by nontreponemal tests .

116
00:13:34,762 --> 00:13:42,962
Traditionally, nontreponemal tests are used first for screening, and results are then confirmed by a treponemal-specific test.

117
00:13:42,962 --> 00:13:49,212
In a newer reverse sequence strategy, screening begins first with a treponemal-specific test.

118
00:13:49,212 --> 00:13:55,887
Each of the serological tests has limitations, including false-positive and negative results.

119
00:13:55,887 --> 00:14:01,912
Management of discordant finding with this latter sequence is shown in Table 68-1.

120
00:14:01,912 --> 00:14:08,237
Notably, both approaches are suitable if a screening, follow-up, and treatment program is in place.

121
00:14:08,237 --> 00:14:10,749
Table 68-1.

122
00:14:10,749 --> 00:14:23,762
Interpretation of Serologic Tests Using Reverse Sequence Syphilis Screening In contrast to these laboratory tests, rapid “point-of-care” (POC) syphilis screening of blood or serum samples is available.

123
00:14:23,762 --> 00:14:28,312
Most tests are treponemalspecific, and positive POC

124
00:14:28,468 --> 00:14:32,655
results can then be confirmed by laboratorybased tests.

125
00:14:32,605 --> 00:14:43,593
In hard-to-reach populations, immediate treatment of women with positive POC results is associated with lower rates of stillbirth, neonatal death, and congenital syphilis .

126
00:14:43,593 --> 00:14:51,905
This practice, however, risks overtreating previously cured women who still have residual persistent treponemal antibodies.

127
00:14:51,905 --> 00:15:00,918
This limitation may be overcome by newer POC dual tests, which simultaneously assess nontreponemal and treponemal antibodies .

128
00:15:00,918 --> 00:15:10,980
Following maternal diagnosis, sonographic evaluation is performed for fetuses >20 weeks’ gestation to search for signs of congenital syphilis.

129
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:23,493
Rac and associates (2014) noted that 31 percent of infected women diagnosed at ≥18 weeks’ gestation had fetuses with abnormal sonographic findings.

130
00:15:23,493 --> 00:15:35,218
Hepatomegaly, placental thickening, hydramnios, ascites, hydrops fetalis, and elevated middle cerebral artery Doppler velocimetry measurements suggest fetal infection.

131
00:15:35,218 --> 00:15:41,705
Before 20 weeks, treatment is highly successful, and abnormal sonographic findings are rare .

132
00:15:41,705 --> 00:15:53,693
With maternal infection and with a fetus of viable age that shows sonographic findings, antepartum fetal heart rate (FHR) monitoring prior to antibiotic treatment is recommended.

133
00:15:53,693 --> 00:15:59,018
If the FHR tracing is reassuring, antibiotic therapy can proceed.

134
00:15:59,018 --> 00:16:11,980
In contrast, spontaneous late decelerations or a nonreactive FHR tracing likely reflects an extremely ill fetus that may poorly tolerate a Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, described subsequently.

135
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:22,768
In this extreme case, consultation with a neonatologist regarding a plan of delaying treatment, pursuing delivery, and then treating in the nursery is a consideration .

136
00:16:22,768 --> 00:16:32,330
After antibiotic treatment, further sonographic surveillance or antenatal testing of fetal well-being is determined in consultation with specialists.

137
00:16:32,330 --> 00:16:42,680
Following adequate treatment, sonographic signs of congenital infection resolve over weeks to months, and delivery is reserved for usual obstetrical indications .

138
00:16:42,680 --> 00:16:51,680
In fetuses without initial signs of congenital syphilis, subsequent sonographic evaluations are reserved for other fetal indications.

139
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:53,755
# Treatment.

140
00:16:53,755 --> 00:17:01,180
Syphilis therapy during pregnancy aims to eradicate maternal infection and to prevent or treat congenital syphilis.

141
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:08,080
Parenteral penicillin G benzathine remains the preferred treatment for all stages of syphilis during pregnancy .

142
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:18,605
A second dose of benzathine penicillin G may be given 1 week after the initial dose in patients with early-stage syphilis or sonographic signs of congenital infection.

143
00:17:18,605 --> 00:17:24,918
This aims to reduce the risk of fetal treatment failure and is our current practice at Parkland Hospital.

144
00:17:24,918 --> 00:17:36,643
High maternal serological titers, preterm delivery, and delivery shortly after antepartum therapy are all risks for failure of maternal treatment to prevent neonatal infection .

145
00:17:36,643 --> 00:17:46,143
Women should abstain from sexual contact for 7 days, until all skin/mucous membrane lesions heal, and until sexual partners are treated.

146
00:17:46,143 --> 00:17:48,655
Table 68-2.

147
00:17:48,655 --> 00:17:56,230
Recommended Treatment for Pregnant Women with Syphilis There are no proven alternatives to penicillin therapy during pregnancy.

148
00:17:56,230 --> 00:18:05,605
Some data support a 1-g daily IV dose of ceftriaxone for 10 days (Cao, 2017; Liang, 2016).

149
00:18:05,605 --> 00:18:13,218
Tetracyclines are effective but not recommended during pregnancy, because of the risk for offspring teeth discoloration.

150
00:18:13,218 --> 00:18:25,268
Following treatment of syphilis before 24 weeks’ gestation, nontreponemal tests are repeated in either the third trimester or no sooner than 8 weeks after treatment unless reinfection is suspected .

151
00:18:25,268 --> 00:18:34,630
Following treatment after 24 weeks, nontreponemal titers may be repeated in the third trimester in some cases and are repeated at delivery.

152
00:18:34,630 --> 00:18:43,718
Adequate serological response to treatment is suggested by a fourfold decline in nontreponemal titer and resolution of any clinical symptoms.

153
00:18:43,718 --> 00:18:52,668
A fourfold decline in titer is more likely to occur following treatment of earlystage syphilis, which typically has high nontreponemal titers.

154
00:18:52,668 --> 00:19:01,780
Absence of a fourfold decline does not itself determine treatment failure, particularly when the initial nontreponemal titer is <1:8.

155
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:05,243
Titers are best interpreted in conjunction with

156
00:19:05,404 --> 00:19:10,591
clinical history and by those experienced with treatment of syphilis in pregnancy.

157
00:19:10,541 --> 00:19:19,454
Because VDRL titers do not correspond directly to RPR titers, consistent use of the same test for surveillance is recommended.

158
00:19:19,454 --> 00:19:27,579
Some successfully treated patients may still exhibit persistently lowlevel positive titers, which are referred to as “serofast.

159
00:19:27,579 --> 00:19:38,704
This state is more likely in older individuals, those with lower initial nontreponemal antibody titers, and those with later stages of syphilis (Seña, 2015).

160
00:19:38,704 --> 00:19:47,441
In some instances, a woman may present without symptoms but describes recent sexual contact with a person who has been diagnosed with syphilis.

161
00:19:47,441 --> 00:19:51,354
She should be evaluated clinically and serologically.

162
00:19:51,354 --> 00:20:01,929
If their sexual contact occurred within the preceding 90 days, the gravida is treated presumptively for early syphilis, even if serological test results are negative.

163
00:20:01,929 --> 00:20:06,129
This accounts for early infection but before seroconversion.

164
00:20:06,129 --> 00:20:12,016
If contact was more than 90 days ago, treatment is based on serological results.

165
00:20:12,016 --> 00:20:24,354
Penicillin Reactions Women with a history of penicillin allergy should have either an oral stepwise penicillin-dose challenge or skin testing performed to confirm the risk of IgE-mediated anaphylaxis.

166
00:20:24,354 --> 00:20:31,691
If confirmed, penicillin desensitization is recommended and followed by benzathine penicillin G treatment .

167
00:20:31,691 --> 00:20:34,241
Table 68-3.

168
00:20:34,241 --> 00:20:50,741
Penicillin Allergy—Oral Desensitization Protocol for Patients with a Positive Skin Test Distinct from allergy, the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can develop following penicillin treatment in most women with primary syphilis and approximately half with secondary infection.

169
00:20:50,741 --> 00:20:59,429
Uterine contractions, mild maternal temperature elevation, decreased fetal movement, and FHR decelerations are findings.

170
00:20:59,429 --> 00:21:05,791
Treatment is supportive with antipyretics as needed, hydration, and oxygen supplementation .

171
00:21:05,791 --> 00:21:15,016
In a study of 50 gravidas who received benzathine penicillin G for syphilis, the incidence of Jarisch-Herxheimer reactions was 40 percent .

172
00:21:15,016 --> 00:21:25,316
Of the 31 women monitored electronically, 42 percent developed regular uterine contractions, and 39 percent developed variable decelerations.

173
00:21:25,316 --> 00:21:29,116
All contractions resolved within 24 hours.

174
00:21:29,116 --> 00:21:48,704
Thus, for fetuses of viable gestational age, consideration is given to administering the first dose of antibiotic in labor and delivery to permit continuous fetal monitoring for at least 24 hours when the fetus has sonographic signs of syphilis (Adhikari, 2020; Wendel, 2002).

175
00:21:48,704 --> 00:22:00,891
With or without sonographic signs of fetal infection, patients are counseled on symptoms of the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction prior to syphilis treatment and are encouraged to seek evaluation if they develop.

176
00:22:00,891 --> 00:22:02,504
Gonorrhea.

177
00:22:02,504 --> 00:22:05,579
# Infection Rates and Effects.

178
00:22:05,579 --> 00:22:12,191
Of reportable bacterial STIs, those caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae are the second most common.

179
00:22:12,191 --> 00:22:21,704
The incidence in women in the United States has continued to rise since 2015, and the highest rates are in those aged 15 to 24 years .

180
00:22:21,704 --> 00:22:29,229
Among women giving birth in 2018, the rate was 310 cases per 100,000 live births .

181
00:22:29,229 --> 00:22:37,979
In most gravidas, infection is limited to the cervix, urethra, and periurethral and greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands.

182
00:22:37,979 --> 00:22:41,516
Acute salpingitis is rare in pregnancy.

183
00:22:41,516 --> 00:22:48,141
However, pregnant women account for a disproportionate number of disseminated gonococcal infections .

184
00:22:48,141 --> 00:22:52,691
Gonococcal infection can have adverse effects in any trimester.

185
00:22:52,691 --> 00:23:05,891
In affected women, associated rates of preterm delivery, preterm prelabor ruptured membranes (PPROM), low birthweight, and perinatal mortality are slightly higher than in those without infection .

186
00:23:05,891 --> 00:23:08,804
Miscarriage rates are not increased.

187
00:23:08,804 --> 00:23:17,066
However, untreated gonococcal cervicitis is associated with septic abortion and with uterine infection after surgical abortion .

188
00:23:17,066 --> 00:23:23,716
Vertical transmission of gonorrhea mainly occurs through fetal contact with cervical infection during birth.

189
00:23:23,716 --> 00:23:32,729
The predominant sequela is gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum, which can lead to corneal scarring, ocular perforation, and blindness.

190
00:23:32,729 --> 00:23:36,791
Transmission rates are high and approximate 40 percent .

191
00:23:36,791 --> 00:23:46,704
Outlined in Chapter 32 (p. 593), ocular prophylaxis is provided to all newborns (U.S. Preventive Services Task Force,

192
00:23:46,852 --> 00:23:50,614
2019a). # Screening and Treatment.

193
00:23:50,564 --> 00:23:57,727
Pregnant women who live in high-prevalence areas or who are at risk for gonorrhea should undergo first-trimester screening.

194
00:23:57,727 --> 00:24:13,364
Risk factors include age ≤25 years, prior or current STI, sex work, a new or nonmonogamous partner, a partner with an STI, multiple sexual partners, and inconsistent condom use if not monogamous .

195
00:24:13,364 --> 00:24:18,239
Gonococcal infection is a marker for concomitant chlamydial infection.

196
00:24:18,239 --> 00:24:25,902
Thus, if chlamydial testing is unavailable, presumptive chlamydial therapy is given to women treated for gonorrhea.

197
00:24:25,902 --> 00:24:30,352
Screening for gonorrhea in women is by culture or NAAT.

198
00:24:30,352 --> 00:24:39,077
NAATs have replaced culture in most laboratories, and kits are available for specific collection from the vagina, endocervix, or urine.

199
00:24:39,077 --> 00:24:47,039
Of these, vaginal or cervical samples are preferred, as urine collection may detect up to 10 percent fewer infections .

200
00:24:47,039 --> 00:24:51,877
If used, the initial urine stream, not midstream, is collected.

201
00:24:51,877 --> 00:24:58,264
In women with vaginal symptoms and who practice anal intercourse, rectal screening can be considered.

202
00:24:58,264 --> 00:25:11,327
One study of nonpregnant women at urban STI clinics found that approximately 20 percent of rectal chlamydia and gonorrhea infections would be missed if only cervical, vaginal, or urine samples were obtained .

203
00:25:11,327 --> 00:25:16,264
NAATs are also suitable for diagnosis of rectal or pharyngeal disease .

204
00:25:16,264 --> 00:25:25,327
An ultrarapid NAAT for gonorrhea and chlamydia has been approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for point-of-care use.

205
00:25:25,327 --> 00:25:37,189
It performs similarly to laboratory-based testing, and results are available in approximately 30 minutes (Food and Drug Administration, 2021; Van Der Pol, 2020).

206
00:25:37,189 --> 00:25:45,764
This test has not been rigorously studied in pregnant women but may reduce barriers to prompt treatment in patients with limited access to care.

207
00:25:45,764 --> 00:25:53,064
Gonorrhea treatment continues to evolve due to the ability of N gonorrhoeae to develop antimicrobial resistance .

208
00:25:53,064 --> 00:25:59,814
In gravidas, if chlamydia has not been excluded, a 1-g oral dose of azithromycin is added.

209
00:25:59,814 --> 00:26:06,339
Patients abstain from intercourse for 7 days after they and their sexual partner(s) have completed treatment.

210
00:26:06,339 --> 00:26:08,939
Table 68-4.

211
00:26:08,939 --> 00:26:17,702
Treatment of Gonorrhea in Pregnancya,b For those with pharyngeal infection, non-ceftriaxone-based regimens have higher failure rates.

212
00:26:17,702 --> 00:26:24,652
Thus, those with contraindications to ceftriaxone merit infectious disease specialist consultation.

213
00:26:24,652 --> 00:26:30,389
With pharyngeal infection, a test-of-cure 1 to 2 weeks after treatment is recommend.

214
00:26:30,389 --> 00:26:35,377
This is unnecessary after treatment of urogenital or rectal gonorrhea.

215
00:26:35,377 --> 00:26:42,627
To exclude reinfection, repeat testing is recommended 3 months after treatment of any gonorrhea infection.

216
00:26:42,627 --> 00:26:59,389
For any woman treated for gonorrhea in the first trimester and for any uninfected woman who is at high risk for gonococcal infection, testing is repeated in the third trimester (American Academy of Pediatrics, 2017; Workowski, 2021).

217
00:26:59,389 --> 00:27:02,002
Sexual contacts are treated.

218
00:27:02,002 --> 00:27:10,339
Expedited therapy, discussed with chlamydial infection, is a less-desirable option due to the now-preferred injectable regimen.

219
00:27:10,339 --> 00:27:16,139
If elected, a single, oral, 800-mg dose of cefixime is provided.

220
00:27:16,139 --> 00:27:24,027
If chlamydia is not excluded, oral doxycycline, 100 mg twice daily for 7 days, is added.

221
00:27:24,027 --> 00:27:27,639
# Disseminated Gonococcal Infections.

222
00:27:27,639 --> 00:27:37,564
Gonococcal bacteremia may cause disseminated infections that manifest as petechial or pustular skin lesions, arthralgias, or septic arthritis.

223
00:27:37,564 --> 00:27:44,164
Antimicrobial susceptibility testing is requested for isolates cultured from infection sites.

224
00:27:44,164 --> 00:27:55,377
For treatment, ceftriaxone, 1 g IV or intramuscularly, is given every 24 hours and continued for 24 to 48 hours after clinical improvement .

225
00:27:55,377 --> 00:28:02,364
An oral agent that is selected based on susceptibility testing is then provided to complete 1 week of therapy.

226
00:28:02,364 --> 00:28:08,989
Prompt recognition and antimicrobial treatment will usually yield favorable outcomes in pregnancy .

227
00:28:08,989 --> 00:28:14,402
Meningitis and endocarditis are rare in pregnancy, but they may be fatal.

228
00:28:14,402 --> 00:28:25,477
For gonococcal endocarditis, ceftriaxone 1 to 2 g IV every 12 hours should be continued for at least 4 weeks, and for meningitis, 10 to 14 days.

229
00:28:25,477 --> 00:28:33,852
In all these cases, a single, 1-g, oral dose of azithromycin is provided to treat potential chlamydial co-infection .

230
00:28:33,852 --> 00:28:35,427
Chlamydial

231
00:28:35,596 --> 00:28:37,308
Infection.

232
00:28:37,258 --> 00:28:40,333
# Infection Rates and Effects.

233
00:28:40,333 --> 00:28:50,096
Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium that attaches to columnar or transitional-cell epithelium to cause urogenital infection.

234
00:28:50,096 --> 00:28:54,421
It is the most common reportable STI in the United States.

235
00:28:54,421 --> 00:29:05,196
The chlamydial infection rate was 694 cases per 100,000 women in 2019, and the highest rate is in those aged 15 to 24 years .

236
00:29:05,196 --> 00:29:13,583
Among women giving birth in 2018, the rate was 1844 cases per 100,000 live births .

237
00:29:13,583 --> 00:29:23,433
Most infected pregnant women are asymptomatic, but a third have urethral syndrome, urethritis, or greater vestibular (Bartholin) gland infection .

238
00:29:23,433 --> 00:29:29,421
Mucopurulent cervicitis may result from chlamydial or gonococcal infection or both.

239
00:29:29,421 --> 00:29:37,458
Uncommon manifestations during pregnancy are endometritis, salpingitis, reactive arthritis, and Reiter syndrome.

240
00:29:37,458 --> 00:29:42,946
The role of chlamydial infection in pregnancy complications remains controversial.

241
00:29:42,946 --> 00:29:57,746
It is disputed whether untreated cervical infection raises rates of preterm delivery, PPROM, low birthweight, or perinatal mortality (Andrews, 2000; Reekie, 2018; Silva, 2011).

242
00:29:57,746 --> 00:30:09,171
Chlamydial infection is not associated with a greater risk of chorioamnionitis or with peripartum pelvic infection (Berman, 1987; Gibbs, 1987).

243
00:30:09,171 --> 00:30:16,508
However, delayed postpartum uterine infection has been described by Hoyme and associates (1986).

244
00:30:16,508 --> 00:30:23,271
The syndrome, which develops 2 to 3 weeks postpartum, is distinct from early postpartum metritis.

245
00:30:23,271 --> 00:30:29,858
It is characterized by vaginal bleeding or discharge, low-grade fever, and uterine tenderness.

246
00:30:29,858 --> 00:30:34,746
Infection at delivery poses a higher risk to the newborn than to the mother.

247
00:30:34,746 --> 00:30:42,246
Vertical transmission leads to infection in 8 to 44 percent of neonates delivered vaginally from affected women .

248
00:30:42,246 --> 00:30:46,783
Of neonatal infections, conjunctivitis is the most common .

249
00:30:46,783 --> 00:30:51,121
Perinatal transmission to newborns can also cause pneumonia.

250
00:30:51,121 --> 00:30:53,783
# Screening and Treatment.

251
00:30:53,783 --> 00:31:07,733
Currently, the USPSTF and the American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend chlamydia screening for all women at the first prenatal visit .

252
00:31:07,733 --> 00:31:22,808
The College further suggests testing in the third trimester for those treated in the first trimester, all women aged ≤25 years, and those aged >25 years with behavioral factors, which mirror those for gonorrhea.

253
00:31:22,808 --> 00:31:31,733
Dionne-Odom and associates (2020) reported a reinfection rate of 10 percent in pregnant women successfully treated before delivery.

254
00:31:31,733 --> 00:31:36,508
Diagnosis is made predominantly by NAAT rather than culture.

255
00:31:36,508 --> 00:31:50,258
Of samples for NAAT, vaginal or cervical samples are preferred, because urine collection may detect up to 10 percent fewer infections (Papp, 2014; Wiesenfeld, 2017).

256
00:31:50,258 --> 00:32:02,483
However, Roberts and associates (2011) evaluated NAAT of urine specimens compared with cervical secretions in more than 2000 pregnant women and found them to be equivalent.

257
00:32:02,483 --> 00:32:07,196
As with gonorrhea, the first portion of the urine stream is collected.

258
00:32:07,196 --> 00:32:13,983
Currently recommended treatment regimens for chlamydial infections are shown in Table 68-5.

259
00:32:13,983 --> 00:32:17,783
Azithromycin is first-line treatment in pregnancy.

260
00:32:17,783 --> 00:32:28,233
The fluoroquinolones and doxycycline are usually avoided in pregnancy, and erythromycin estolate is contraindicated because of drug-related hepatotoxicity.

261
00:32:28,233 --> 00:32:30,946
Table 68-5.

262
00:32:30,946 --> 00:32:39,746
Oral Treatment of Chlamydia trachomatis Infection in Pregnancya A test of cure is repeated 4 weeks after therapy completion in pregnancy.

263
00:32:39,746 --> 00:32:44,171
Earlier NAAT may resample dead but treated organisms .

264
00:32:44,171 --> 00:32:52,658
A positive result can reflect a reinfection, a false-positive case from dead organisms, or treatment failure or noncompliance.

265
00:32:52,658 --> 00:32:57,121
Fortunately, drug resistance is rare for C trachomatis .

266
00:32:57,121 --> 00:33:01,733
Our practice is to retreat women with positive test-of-cure results.

267
00:33:01,733 --> 00:33:07,996
Additionally, repeat screening 3 months after treatment is recommended to exclude reinfection .

268
00:33:07,996 --> 00:33:11,246
# Expedited Partner Therapy.

269
00:33:11,246 --> 00:33:28,508
To prevent STI transmission, guidelines for expedited partner therapy (EPT) have been created by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2006a) and are endorsed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

270
00:33:28,508 --> 00:33:35,908
With EPT, treatment is provided to persons who were sexual contacts of the patient within the prior 60 days.

271
00:33:35,908 --> 00:33:37,371
Partner

272
00:33:37,540 --> 00:33:49,252
therapy may be a prescription slip for the partner, direct medication administration to the partner and patient simultaneously, or a medication packet delivered to the partner(s) by the patient.

273
00:33:49,202 --> 00:33:58,302
Ideally, EPT does not replace traditional partner referral to allow their medical assessment, counseling, and screening for other STIs.

274
00:33:58,302 --> 00:34:03,815
EPT is acceptable for treatment of sexual contacts with chlamydial infection.

275
00:34:03,815 --> 00:34:14,765
Because of new guidelines that recommend injectable ceftriaxone alone, EPT for gonorrhea is less desirable unless the partner(s) will be unable or unwilling to seek treatment .

276
00:34:14,765 --> 00:34:23,477
Fewer data are available to assess this strategy for trichomoniasis (Kissinger, 2006; Schwebke, 2010).

277
00:34:23,477 --> 00:34:26,840
EPT is not recommended for syphilis.

278
00:34:26,840 --> 00:34:31,890
EPT is legally permissible or potentially allowable in all states.

279
00:34:31,890 --> 00:34:42,790
Links to specific state laws are found at: www.cdc.gov/std/ept/legal/default.htm.

280
00:34:42,790 --> 00:34:57,852
Notably, the risk of litigation following an adverse outcome may be elevated when a practice has uncertain legal status or is outside formally accepted community practice standards Medication-related adverse reactions among treated partners is rare .

281
00:34:57,852 --> 00:35:04,740
The goal of EPT in pregnancy is to lower reinfection rates and adverse maternal and neonatal effects.

282
00:35:04,740 --> 00:35:12,327
A recent study at Parkland Hospital showed that EPT did not lower the chlamydia reinfection rate prior to delivery .

283
00:35:12,327 --> 00:35:20,465
Partner compliance, cost, and education may be factors (McCool-Myers, 2020; Slutsker, 2020).

284
00:35:20,465 --> 00:35:22,902
Herpes Simplex Virus.

285
00:35:22,902 --> 00:35:25,327
# Adult Disease.

286
00:35:25,327 --> 00:35:31,190
Orogenital HSV infection manifests as shallow ulcers on affected mucosa.

287
00:35:31,190 --> 00:35:38,427
Infection stems from either of two different HSV viruses that are distinguished based on immunological differences.

288
00:35:38,427 --> 00:35:47,765
Yet, the two viruses have significant DNA homology, and thus prior infection with one type attenuates a primary infection with the other.

289
00:35:47,765 --> 00:35:54,952
Type 2 HSV is recovered almost exclusively from the genital tract and is usually transmitted sexually.

290
00:35:54,952 --> 00:36:01,390
Type 1 is responsible for most nongenital infections and typically is acquired in childhood.

291
00:36:01,390 --> 00:36:09,965
However, more than half of the new cases of genital herpes in adolescents and young adults are now caused by HSV-1 infection .

292
00:36:09,965 --> 00:36:18,227
This rise in the prevalence of HSV-1 genital disease may stem from an increase in rates of oral-genital sexual practices.

293
00:36:18,227 --> 00:36:26,652
Another explanation is that HSV-1 acquisition has declined in childhood as a result of improved living conditions and hygiene .

294
00:36:26,652 --> 00:36:36,490
Without prior exposure, young people without HSV-1 antibodies are rendered susceptible to genital acquisition of HSV-1 or -2.

295
00:36:36,490 --> 00:36:44,015
Genital herpes simplex virus affects an estimated 19 million adolescents and adults in the United States .

296
00:36:44,015 --> 00:36:54,902
In pregnant women, the HSV-2 seroprevalence was 21 percent, and for HSV-1, it was 59 percent from 2007 to 2014.

297
00:36:54,902 --> 00:37:04,602
However, from 1999 to 2014, pregnant women with fewer sex partners were increasingly seronegative for both virus types .

298
00:37:04,602 --> 00:37:15,302
Seronegative pregnant women have a 4 to 5 percent risk to acquire either serotype during pregnancy (Brown, 1997; Kulhanjian, 1992).

299
00:37:15,302 --> 00:37:23,177
For those who are already HSV-1 seropositive, acquisition risk for HSV-2 approximates 2 percent .

300
00:37:23,177 --> 00:37:28,127
Once transmitted by contact, viruses replicate at the entry site.

301
00:37:28,127 --> 00:37:34,227
Following mucocutaneous infection, the virus moves retrograde along sensory nerves.

302
00:37:34,227 --> 00:37:40,340
It remains latent in cranial nerves or dorsal spinal ganglia, but recurrences are common.

303
00:37:40,340 --> 00:37:44,652
HSV infections may be categorized into three groups.

304
00:37:44,652 --> 00:37:56,090
First-episode primary infection describes the case in which HSV-1 or 2 is isolated from a lesion in the absence of HSV-1 or -2 serological antibodies.

305
00:37:56,090 --> 00:38:05,340
The typical incubation period of 6 to 8 days may be followed by a papular eruption with itching or tingling that then becomes painful and vesicular.

306
00:38:05,340 --> 00:38:10,727
Multiple vulvar and perineal lesions may or may not coalesce but then ulcerate .

307
00:38:10,727 --> 00:38:14,515
Associated inguinal adenopathy can be severe.

308
00:38:14,515 --> 00:38:21,377
Many women do not present with typical lesions, and abraded or knife-cut lesions instead may be found.

309
00:38:21,377 --> 00:38:26,152
Cervical involvement is common, although it may be unapparent clinically.

310
00:38:26,152 --> 00:38:33,865
Transient systemic influenza-like symptoms or isolated high fevers with or without painful lesions are frequent and

311
00:38:34,036 --> 00:38:36,398
attributed to viremia.

312
00:38:36,348 --> 00:38:43,798
Uncommonly, women require hospitalization for febrile syndrome or for pain control of widespread ulceration.

313
00:38:43,798 --> 00:38:50,723
Hepatitis, encephalitis, or pneumonia infrequently develop, and disseminated disease is rare.

314
00:38:50,723 --> 00:38:55,798
After 2 to 4 weeks, all signs and symptoms of infection disappear.

315
00:38:55,798 --> 00:39:04,948
In contrast to these classic symptoms, the percentage of asymptomatic primary HSV-2 genital infections may reach 90 percent .

316
00:39:04,948 --> 00:39:07,673
Figure 68-4.

317
00:39:07,673 --> 00:39:12,448
First-episode primary genital herpes simplex virus infection.

318
00:39:12,448 --> 00:39:19,048
As shown by the arrows, lesions may be vesicular, may be crusted, or may be shallow ulcers.

319
00:39:19,048 --> 00:39:29,461
First-episode nonprimary infection is diagnosed when one HSV type is isolated from a lesion in a woman who has only antibodies to the other HSV serotype.

320
00:39:29,461 --> 00:39:39,586
For example, prior childhood HSV-1 infection likely provides cross-reacting antibodies to blunt the effects of new HSV-2 acquisition.

321
00:39:39,586 --> 00:39:50,823
Compared with primary infection, nonprimary infections produce fewer systemic manifestations or lesions, less pain, and briefer duration of lesions and viral shedding.

322
00:39:50,823 --> 00:40:00,661
Recurrent disease is characterized by isolation of HSV-1 or -2 from the genital tract in women who carry antibodies to the same serotype.

323
00:40:00,661 --> 00:40:10,286
During the latency period, in which viral particles reside in nerve ganglia, reactivation is common and mediated through poorly understood stimuli.

324
00:40:10,286 --> 00:40:18,586
In general, lesions are fewer in number, are less tender, and shed virus for a shorter period than those of primary infection.

325
00:40:18,586 --> 00:40:21,773
Typically, they recur at the same sites.

326
00:40:21,773 --> 00:40:29,061
Recurrences are most frequent in the first year after initial infection, and rates slowly decline subsequently .

327
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:35,086
Gravidas with a known prior history of genital HSV often experience recurrences .

328
00:40:35,086 --> 00:40:40,298
Asymptomatic viral shedding is defined by the absence of clinical findings.

329
00:40:40,298 --> 00:40:50,561
Most infected women shed virus intermittently over time, and most HSV transmission to a partner occurs during these periods of asymptomatic viral shedding.

330
00:40:50,561 --> 00:40:53,298
# Vertical Transmission.

331
00:40:53,298 --> 00:40:57,573
HSV poses a higher risk to the newborn than to the mother.

332
00:40:57,573 --> 00:41:11,698
Thus, strategies aim to curb rates of vertical transmission, which may occur by three routes: (1) peripartum in 85 percent, (2) postnatal in 10 percent, or (3) intrauterine in 5 percent .

333
00:41:11,698 --> 00:41:17,536
Evidence does not suggest an obvious link between HSV infection and miscarriage .

334
00:41:17,536 --> 00:41:26,173
Peripartum transmission is by far the more frequent route of infection, and the fetus is exposed to virus shed from the lower genital tract.

335
00:41:26,173 --> 00:41:33,761
HSV-1 or -2 invades the uterus following membrane rupture or is transmitted by contact at delivery.

336
00:41:33,761 --> 00:41:43,723
The newborn is mainly infected, but rare cases of maternal endometritis have been described (Hollier, 1997; McGill, 2012).

337
00:41:43,723 --> 00:41:53,723
Of neonatal manifestations, infection may localize to the skin, eye, or mouth—SEM disease—in approximately 40 percent of cases.

338
00:41:53,723 --> 00:42:00,623
Instead, central nervous system (CNS) disease with encephalitis is seen in 30 percent.

339
00:42:00,623 --> 00:42:07,036
Last, disseminated disease that involves multiple major organs is found in 32 percent.

340
00:42:07,036 --> 00:42:11,511
Localized infection is usually associated with a good outcome.

341
00:42:11,511 --> 00:42:22,936
Conversely, even with acyclovir treatment, disseminated infection has a mortality rate of nearly 30 percent (Corey, 2009; Kimberlin, 2011).

342
00:42:22,936 --> 00:42:31,511
Of disseminated or cerebral infection survivors, serious developmental and CNS morbidity is seen in 20 to 50 percent.

343
00:42:31,511 --> 00:42:42,323
In the United States, the neonatal infection rate ranges from 1 to 5 cases in 10,000 births (Donda, 2019; Mahant, 2019).

344
00:42:42,323 --> 00:42:48,411
Most infected newborns are born to mothers with no reported history of HSV infection .

345
00:42:48,411 --> 00:43:02,736
The risk of neonatal infection correlates with the presence of HSV in the genital tract, HSV type, invasive obstetrical procedures, and stage of maternal infection (Brown, 2005, 2007).

346
00:43:02,736 --> 00:43:10,848
Neonates born to women who acquire genital HSV near the time of delivery have a 30- to 50-percent risk of infection.

347
00:43:10,848 --> 00:43:19,811
This is attributed to higher viral loads and the lack of transplacental protective antibodies (Brown, 1997, 2000).

348
00:43:19,811 --> 00:43:29,323
With recurrent HSV, the neonatal infection rate is <1 percent (Pasternak, 2010; Prober, 1987).

349
00:43:29,323 --> 00:43:35,398
Postpartum transmission is uncommon and passed to the newborn by contact with an infected mother,

350
00:43:35,548 --> 00:43:38,573
family member, or health-care worker.

351
00:43:38,523 --> 00:43:43,010
The clinical presentation mirrors that with peripartum transmission.

352
00:43:43,010 --> 00:43:55,748
In-utero transmission of HSV-1 or HSV-2 is rare and is part of the TORCH (toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes virus) group of infections.

353
00:43:55,748 --> 00:44:11,085
Intrauterine HSV infection classically leads to disease involving the skin (blisters, scarring), the CNS (hydranencephaly, microcephaly, intracranial calcification), or the eyes (chorioretinitis, microphthalmia).

354
00:44:11,085 --> 00:44:13,835
Bone and viscera can be involved .

355
00:44:13,835 --> 00:44:27,260
Congenital infection with HSV or another TORCH infection is considered in a fetus with early or severe growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial anomalies or calcifications, or hepatomegaly.

356
00:44:27,260 --> 00:44:35,998
If sonographic findings are identified, amniocentesis is recommended, and maternal HSV serological testing is done.

357
00:44:35,998 --> 00:44:44,673
Analysis of amnionic fluid for HSV-1 and HSV-2 serotypes is by NAAT (PCR) and culture .

358
00:44:44,673 --> 00:44:47,073
# Diagnosis.

359
00:44:47,073 --> 00:45:00,685
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d) and USPSTF (2016) recommend against routine serological HSV screening in asymptomatic gravidas.

360
00:45:00,685 --> 00:45:08,698
However, for a woman with a reported history of genital or oral lesions, a serological HSV diagnosis is sought.

361
00:45:08,698 --> 00:45:21,835
For women with either suspicious ulcers or sonographic findings suggestive of congenital infection, direct virological testing, described next, is performed along with serological testing for HSV.

362
00:45:21,835 --> 00:45:27,698
Testing for syphilis and other STIs is also considered for women with genital ulcers.

363
00:45:27,698 --> 00:45:37,385
Direct virological tests, which are either PCR or culture, are performed on a specimen from a mucocutaneous lesion or from amniocentesis.

364
00:45:37,385 --> 00:45:47,598
For mucocutaneous lesions, PCR assays are more sensitive, the results generally are available in 1 to 2 days, and specimen handling is easier.

365
00:45:47,598 --> 00:45:55,685
The sensitivity of culture for detecting HSV is relatively low as vesicular lesions begin to ulcerate and then crust.

366
00:45:55,685 --> 00:46:00,585
Also, results sometimes are not available for 7 to 14 days .

367
00:46:00,585 --> 00:46:06,260
Regardless of the test performed, HSV viral types should be differentiated .

368
00:46:06,260 --> 00:46:11,873
Importantly, a negative culture or PCR result does not exclude infection.

369
00:46:11,873 --> 00:46:15,660
In contrast, false-positive results are rare.

370
00:46:15,660 --> 00:46:21,235
For amnionic fluid assessment, both culture and PCR are recommended .

371
00:46:21,235 --> 00:46:29,560
Serological assays detect antibodies produced against the specific HSV glycoproteins, G1 and G2.

372
00:46:29,560 --> 00:46:40,910
These proteins evoke type-specific antibody responses to HSV-1 and HSV-2 infection, respectively, and they reliably differentiate the two serotypes.

373
00:46:40,910 --> 00:46:48,160
Antibody detection confirms clinical infection and may reflect a carrier state in an asymptomatic patient.

374
00:46:48,160 --> 00:46:54,323
IgG antibodies develop 1 to 2 weeks after a primary infection and then persist.

375
00:46:54,323 --> 00:47:02,298
Serological testing may be falsely negative in the case of early infection but may also establish a nonprimary infection.

376
00:47:02,298 --> 00:47:12,423
For this reason, direct detection of the virus in a lesion with concomitant type-specific serological testing is helpful for clinical interpretation and counseling.

377
00:47:12,423 --> 00:47:19,560
Sensitivity and specificity of glycoprotein-based IgG tests approach 90 to 100 percent .

378
00:47:19,560 --> 00:47:26,985
IgM antibody detection is not a useful test, and antibodies may be detected with recurrent episodes.

379
00:47:26,985 --> 00:47:29,210
# Management.

380
00:47:29,210 --> 00:47:39,510
In nonpregnant patients, antiviral therapy with acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir is used to treat first-episode orogenital herpes.

381
00:47:39,510 --> 00:47:45,560
Oral or parenteral preparations attenuate clinical infection and viral shedding duration.

382
00:47:45,560 --> 00:47:52,610
Chronic suppressive therapy also is an option to limit recurrences and to reduce heterosexual transmission .

383
00:47:52,610 --> 00:48:00,773
In pregnant women, acyclovir and valacyclovir are safe (Briggs, 2022; Stone, 2004).

384
00:48:00,773 --> 00:48:05,073
Data are limited regarding fetal effects with famciclovir.

385
00:48:05,073 --> 00:48:14,335
For a primary outbreak during pregnancy, women may be given antiviral therapy to attenuate and decrease the duration of symptoms and viral shedding .

386
00:48:14,335 --> 00:48:19,573
Women with HIV co-infection may require a longer duration of treatment.

387
00:48:19,573 --> 00:48:30,685
Those with severe or disseminated HSV are given IV acyclovir, 5 to 10 mg/kg every 8 hours for 2 to 7 days until clinically improved.

388
00:48:30,685 --> 00:48:36,535
This is followed by oral antiviral drugs to complete at least 10 days of total therapy .

389
00:48:36,535 --> 00:48:40,185
For intense discomfort, oral analgesics

390
00:48:40,348 --> 00:48:44,223
and topical lidocaine ointment may provide some relief.

391
00:48:44,173 --> 00:48:50,410
Comorbid urinary retention is rare but can be treated briefly with an indwelling bladder catheter.

392
00:48:50,410 --> 00:48:53,160
Table 68-6.

393
00:48:53,160 --> 00:49:04,710
Oral Antiviral Medications for Herpesvirus Infection in Pregnancya For recurrent HSV infections during pregnancy, antiviral treatment is provided mainly for symptom relief .

394
00:49:04,710 --> 00:49:12,060
Although uncommon, acyclovir resistance has been reported, predominantly in immunocompromised patients .

395
00:49:12,060 --> 00:49:23,848
Of precautions during pregnancy, amniocentesis, percutaneous cord blood sampling, or transabdominal chorionic villus sampling may be performed even with active genital lesions.

396
00:49:23,848 --> 00:49:28,773
Transcervical procedures may best be delayed until lesions have resolved .

397
00:49:28,773 --> 00:49:32,323
# Peripartum Shedding Prophylaxis.

398
00:49:32,323 --> 00:49:41,160
To diminish vertical transmission risks, cesarean delivery is indicated for women with active genital lesions or prodromal symptoms.

399
00:49:41,160 --> 00:49:53,673
Several studies have shown that acyclovir or valacyclovir suppression initiated at 36 weeks’ gestation for gravidas with recurrences during pregnancy lowers the number of HSV outbreaks at term.

400
00:49:53,673 --> 00:49:57,460
The goal is to decrease the need for cesarean delivery .

401
00:49:57,460 --> 00:50:06,848
This suppressive therapy will also decrease viral shedding (Scott, 2002; Sheffield, 2006; Watts, 2003).

402
00:50:06,848 --> 00:50:16,560
One systematic review evaluated acyclovir prophylaxis given from 36 weeks to delivery to women with HSV recurrence during pregnancy.

403
00:50:16,560 --> 00:50:32,473
Sheffield and colleagues (2003) found that suppressive therapy was associated with significantly lower rates of clinical HSV recurrence, cesarean delivery for HSV recurrences, total HSV detection, and asymptomatic shedding.

404
00:50:32,473 --> 00:50:41,410
Subsequent studies using valacyclovir suppression showed similar results (Andrews, 2006; Sheffield, 2006).

405
00:50:41,410 --> 00:50:56,960
Because of these studies, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d) recommends viral suppressive therapy to begin at ≥36 weeks’ gestation for women with a clinical history of genital herpes.

406
00:50:56,960 --> 00:50:59,973
Suppression continues until delivery.

407
00:50:59,973 --> 00:51:08,310
Notably, despite maternal antiviral suppression, several cases of atypical neonatal herpes infection have been reported .

408
00:51:08,310 --> 00:51:16,073
On presentation for delivery, a woman with a history of HSV should be questioned regarding current prodromal symptoms.

409
00:51:16,073 --> 00:51:25,685
A careful examination of the vulva, vagina, and cervix is performed for all, and women without genital lesions may proceed with labor and delivery.

410
00:51:25,685 --> 00:51:34,848
Usual obstetrical management, including invasive fetal monitoring, is supported in the absence of active lesions or prodromal symptoms .

411
00:51:34,848 --> 00:51:40,123
Suspicious lesions should be swabbed for culture or NAAT evaluation.

412
00:51:40,123 --> 00:51:46,748
Cesarean delivery is indicated for women with suspicious genital lesions or prodromal symptoms.

413
00:51:46,748 --> 00:51:57,373
Moreover, for women with a clinical recurrence at delivery, there is no absolute duration of membrane rupture beyond which the fetus would not benefit from cesarean delivery .

414
00:51:57,373 --> 00:52:06,135
Cesarean delivery is not recommended for women with a history of HSV infection but no active genital disease at the time of delivery.

415
00:52:06,135 --> 00:52:14,285
Moreover, an active lesion in a nongenital area can be covered by an occlusive dressing, and vaginal delivery is allowed.

416
00:52:14,285 --> 00:52:20,885
With PPROM, no evidence suggests that external lesions cause ascending fetal infection.

417
00:52:20,885 --> 00:52:25,823
However, antiviral treatment during expectant care is recommended.

418
00:52:25,823 --> 00:52:35,185
Major and associates (2003) described expectant management in 29 such women at gestational ages <31 weeks.

419
00:52:35,185 --> 00:52:42,585
No cases of neonatal HSV developed, and the maximum infection risk was calculated to be 10 percent.

420
00:52:42,585 --> 00:52:48,235
Women with active HSV lesions may breastfeed if the breast skin has no lesions.

421
00:52:48,235 --> 00:52:50,935
Strict hand washing is essential.

422
00:52:50,935 --> 00:53:00,185
Valacyclovir and acyclovir may be used for symptomatic maternal lesions during breastfeeding, as drug concentrations in breast milk are low .

423
00:53:00,185 --> 00:53:02,610
Human Papillomavirus.

424
00:53:02,610 --> 00:53:05,985
# Maternal Infection and Prevention.

425
00:53:05,985 --> 00:53:11,710
This is a common STI, and numerous serotypes infect the lower genital tract.

426
00:53:11,710 --> 00:53:21,835
In the United States from 2005 to 2006, the overall HPV prevalence was 40 percent in females aged 14 to 59 years .

427
00:53:21,835 --> 00:53:29,598
Prevalence is highest in younger women, and some of this seroprevalence now reflects HPV vaccination in this age group .

428
00:53:29,598 --> 00:53:32,723
Reproductive-aged women often become

429
00:53:32,884 --> 00:53:40,446
infected within a few years of sexual activity onset, and most infections are asymptomatic and transient.

430
00:53:40,396 --> 00:53:44,459
High-risk types are those with the most oncogenic potential.

431
00:53:44,459 --> 00:53:51,659
Of these, HPV types 16 and 18 are often associated with lower reproductive tract dysplasia .

432
00:53:51,659 --> 00:53:58,859
Mucocutaneous genital warts termed condyloma acuminata are usually caused by types 6 and 11.

433
00:53:58,859 --> 00:54:06,996
The clear link between HPV infection and cervical neoplasia led to development of vaccines that reduce cervical cancer risk .

434
00:54:06,996 --> 00:54:13,409
Although three vaccines are licensed, only one vaccine is currently available in the United States.

435
00:54:13,409 --> 00:54:25,521
Gardasil 9 is a nonavalent HPV vaccine against serotypes 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 35, 45, 52, and 58.

436
00:54:25,521 --> 00:54:29,109
This vaccine is not recommended for gravidas.

437
00:54:29,109 --> 00:54:41,671
However, inadvertent exposures do occur, and no adverse pregnancy outcomes are associated (Kharbanda, 2021; Moreira, 2016; Scheller, 2017).

438
00:54:41,671 --> 00:54:49,446
If a woman is found to be pregnant after starting the vaccination series, remaining doses are delayed and given after delivery.

439
00:54:49,446 --> 00:54:52,721
Women who are breastfeeding may be vaccinated.

440
00:54:52,721 --> 00:55:00,146
Gardasil is licensed for females aged 9 to 45 years, and the target age is 11 to 12 years.

441
00:55:00,146 --> 00:55:04,209
It is recommended for those aged 9 to 26 years.

442
00:55:04,209 --> 00:55:12,684
It is suitable for those aged 27 to 45 years, after shared decision-making regarding age-related benefits and risks .

443
00:55:12,684 --> 00:55:22,921
The three-dose series is given on a schedule of 0, 2, and 6 months for those aged older than 15 (Table 10-7, p. 189).

444
00:55:22,921 --> 00:55:26,734
A two-dose regimen is used for younger individuals.

445
00:55:26,734 --> 00:55:30,121
# Condyloma Acuminata Treatment.

446
00:55:30,121 --> 00:55:34,496
Genital warts frequently grow in number and size with pregnancy.

447
00:55:34,496 --> 00:55:39,059
They commonly undergo apoptosis and resolution postpartum.

448
00:55:39,059 --> 00:55:46,609
Rarely, lesions may fill the vagina or cover the perineum and make vaginal delivery or episiotomy difficult.

449
00:55:46,609 --> 00:55:52,871
Genital wart eradication during pregnancy is usually unnecessary unless they are symptomatic.

450
00:55:52,871 --> 00:56:00,071
Therapy is directed toward debulking symptomatic warts yet minimizing treatment toxicity to the mother and fetus.

451
00:56:00,071 --> 00:56:09,609
Trichloroacetic or bichloracetic acid in an 80- to 90-percent solution applied topically weekly is an effective regimen for external warts.

452
00:56:09,609 --> 00:56:14,609
Some prefer cryotherapy, laser ablation, or surgical excision.

453
00:56:14,609 --> 00:56:25,934
Agents that have limited safety data and thus are not recommended in pregnancy include podophyllin resin, podofilox solution or gel, imiquimod cream, and sinecatechins.

454
00:56:25,934 --> 00:56:28,684
# Neonatal Infection.

455
00:56:28,684 --> 00:56:33,121
Vertical transmission rates of HPV to the newborn are minimal.

456
00:56:33,121 --> 00:56:41,146
Juvenileonset recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (JoRRP) is a rare, benign neoplasm of the larynx.

457
00:56:41,146 --> 00:56:48,471
It can cause hoarseness and respiratory distress in children and is most often caused by HPV 6 or 11.

458
00:56:48,471 --> 00:56:54,084
Risks for infection are maternal genital HPV infection and longer labors .

459
00:56:54,084 --> 00:56:59,884
Although many newborns are likely exposed to HPV, few develop JoRRP .

460
00:56:59,884 --> 00:57:09,634
The incidence in 2013 in the United States was 5 cases in 10,000 births, and dropping rates are attributed to HPV vaccination .

461
00:57:09,634 --> 00:57:17,146
The benefit of cesarean delivery for prevention is unknown, and it is not recommended to avert HPV transmission.

462
00:57:17,146 --> 00:57:19,059
Vaginitis.

463
00:57:19,059 --> 00:57:23,459
Pregnant women frequently develop increased vaginal discharge.

464
00:57:23,459 --> 00:57:34,784
This may be a physiological discharge, described in Chapter 4 (p. 54), but should be differentiated from symptomatic vaginitis, which is also common in pregnancy.

465
00:57:34,784 --> 00:57:39,559
Fortunately, normal vaginal flora serve to prevent vaginitis.

466
00:57:39,559 --> 00:57:48,909
To better understand this, normal vaginal microflora composition and function are currently being studied in the Vaginal Human Microbiome Project .

467
00:57:48,909 --> 00:57:51,921
# Bacterial Vaginosis.

468
00:57:51,921 --> 00:57:58,446
Diagnosis Bacterial vaginosis (BV) is a maldistribution of normal vaginal flora.

469
00:57:58,446 --> 00:58:05,709
With BV, numbers of lactobacilli are decreased, and anaerobic bacterial species are overrepresented.

470
00:58:05,709 --> 00:58:20,609
These anaerobes include Gardnerella, Prevotella, Mobiluncus, and Bacteroides species; Atopobium vaginae; and BVassociated bacteria, provisionally named BVAB1, BVAB2, and BVAB3.

471
00:58:20,609 --> 00:58:25,109
These last three are relatively newly recognized bacteria .

472
00:58:25,109 --> 00:58:34,171
Ribosomal RNA gene-sequencing techniques have identified composite communities of vaginal flora, also called the vaginal microbiota.

473
00:58:34,171 --> 00:58:39,796
Five types of these composite communities exist and are referred to as community state

474
00:58:39,964 --> 00:58:42,276
types (CSTs).

475
00:58:42,226 --> 00:58:48,876
A woman can be categorized to one of these five CSTs based on her vaginal microbiota composition .

476
00:58:48,876 --> 00:58:54,764
Researchers have begun to quantify the risk of BV associated with these CST groups.

477
00:58:54,764 --> 00:59:01,039
Specifically, CSTs I, II, III, and V are rich in lactobacilli.

478
00:59:01,039 --> 00:59:13,951
CST IV is a heterogeneous microbiota of strict anaerobes and is associated with BV. CSTs vary racially, and CST IV is the most common in asymptomatic, healthy black women .

479
00:59:13,951 --> 00:59:24,351
Pregnancy-related changes in vaginal microbiota also are being defined and may hold keys to adverse BV-related pregnancy outcomes, discussed subsequently .

480
00:59:24,351 --> 00:59:30,214
Of childbearing-aged women in the United States, nearly 30 percent have BV.

481
00:59:30,214 --> 00:59:34,376
In black women, the prevalence approximates 50 percent .

482
00:59:34,376 --> 00:59:37,114
Most women are asymptomatic.

483
00:59:37,114 --> 00:59:42,676
In those with symptoms, a foul, thin vaginal discharge is a typical complaint.

484
00:59:42,676 --> 00:59:54,839
Associated risk factors are douching, multiple partners, smoking, and altered host immunity (Desseauve, 2012; Koumans, 2007; Murphy, 2016).

485
00:59:54,839 --> 01:00:19,489
For clinical diagnosis of BV, three of the four following Amsel criteria are present: (1) vaginal pH >4.5; (2) a thin, milky, noninflammatory vaginal discharge; (3) >20 percent clue cells seen microscopically; and (4) a fishy odor after addition of 10-percent potassium hydroxide to a vaginal secretion sample .

486
01:00:19,489 --> 01:00:23,351
The last is described as a positive “whiff test” result.

487
01:00:23,351 --> 01:00:32,351
Likewise, alkalinity of seminal fluid and blood are responsible for complaints of foul odor after intercourse and with menses in affected women.

488
01:00:32,351 --> 01:00:42,626
Initially, a vaginal fluid sample is mixed with saline on a glass slide for microscopic evaluation, and this is often called a wet preparation or “wet prep.

489
01:00:42,626 --> 01:00:50,976
Clue cells are vaginal epithelial cells containing many attached bacteria, which create a poorly defined stippled cellular border .

490
01:00:50,976 --> 01:00:55,014
Microscopically, leukocyte numbers are not increased.

491
01:00:55,014 --> 01:01:02,076
The higher vaginal pH stems from the diminished acid production due to a diminished lactobacilli population.

492
01:01:02,076 --> 01:01:10,026
Notably, Trichomonas vaginalis infection also is associated with anaerobic overgrowth and elaborated amines.

493
01:01:10,026 --> 01:01:16,239
Thus, women diagnosed with BV should have no microscopic evidence of trichomoniasis.

494
01:01:16,239 --> 01:01:18,976
Figure 68-5.

495
01:01:18,976 --> 01:01:21,701
A. Bacterial vaginosis.

496
01:01:21,701 --> 01:01:26,801
Microscopy reveals several squamous cells heavily studded with bacteria.

497
01:01:26,801 --> 01:01:33,264
Clue cells are covered to the extent that cell borders are blurred and nuclei are not visible (arrows).

498
01:01:33,264 --> 01:01:36,251
B. Trichomonads (arrows).

499
01:01:36,251 --> 01:01:44,039
The Nugent score, used primarily in research rather than clinical practice, is a system employed for diagnosing BV .

500
01:01:44,039 --> 01:01:53,439
During microscopic examination of a gram-stained vaginal discharge smear, scores are calculated by assessing bacteria staining and morphology.

501
01:01:53,439 --> 01:02:01,164
NAATs to detect the bacterial types found in women with BV have suitable accuracy and may be used for symptomatic women .

502
01:02:01,164 --> 01:02:08,164
However, these tests assess for bacteria that may also be part of normal flora in asymptomatic women.

503
01:02:08,164 --> 01:02:16,914
Moreover, compared with traditional methods, molecular tests have yet to show superior health outcomes but do add substantial cost.

504
01:02:16,914 --> 01:02:30,001
Treatment Adverse pregnancy-related health outcomes associated with BV are preterm birth, PPROM, and postpartum endometritis (Hillier, 1995; Watts, 1990).

505
01:02:30,001 --> 01:02:38,864
It also increases susceptibility to STIs, including HIV (Atashili, 2008; Brotman, 2010).

506
01:02:38,864 --> 01:02:49,839
For women at low risk for preterm birth, however, treatment of BV does not reduce preterm birth rates (Brocklehurst, 2013; Carey, 2000).

507
01:02:49,839 --> 01:03:03,864
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) and USPSTF (2020) do not recommend routine BV screening of asymptomatic gravidas at low risk for preterm delivery.

508
01:03:03,864 --> 01:03:09,939
For women with prior preterm birth, evidence of screening and treatment benefits are conflicting .

509
01:03:09,939 --> 01:03:16,551
At Parkland Hospital, we do not routinely screen for BV among women with prior preterm birth.

510
01:03:16,551 --> 01:03:19,751
Treatment is reserved for symptomatic women .

511
01:03:19,751 --> 01:03:25,801
Agents not listed are best avoided due to insufficient data regarding their use in pregnancy .

512
01:03:25,801 --> 01:03:38,201
It is still debated whether BV is a sexually transmitted infection, and treatment of a male partner does not lower recurrence rates (Amaya-Guio, 2016; Schwebke, 2021).

513
01:03:38,201 --> 01:03:40,889
Table 68-7.

514
01:03:40,889 --> 01:03:42,614
Treatment of

515
01:03:42,772 --> 01:03:47,609
Bacterial Vaginosis in Pregnancya # Trichomoniasis.

516
01:03:47,559 --> 01:03:56,709
Diagnosis Vaginitis caused by Trichomonas vaginalis is common, and its prevalence in the United States approximates 2 percent in women .

517
01:03:56,709 --> 01:04:01,609
The prevalence is higher in those older than 30 years compared with younger women.

518
01:04:01,609 --> 01:04:07,497
Risks include black race, douching, and greater number of lifetime sexual partners .

519
01:04:07,497 --> 01:04:13,834
Among women, frequent sites of infection include the urethra, endocervix, and vagina.

520
01:04:13,834 --> 01:04:22,972
Those with symptomatic vaginitis often have a purulent discharge, pruritus, vulvovaginal erythema, and colpitis macularis.

521
01:04:22,972 --> 01:04:29,959
The last is often termed a “strawberry cervix” and displays a patchy, maculoerythematous ectocervix.

522
01:04:29,959 --> 01:04:36,597
Trichomonads are flagellated, pear-shaped, motile organisms that are somewhat larger than leukocytes.

523
01:04:36,597 --> 01:04:42,509
These parasites can readily be seen microscopically moving briskly in wet preparation.

524
01:04:42,509 --> 01:04:48,772
Prompt inspection of vaginal secretions is advantageous because trichomonads slow with cooling.

525
01:04:48,772 --> 01:04:54,309
At times, T vaginalis may be found incidentally on a Pap test slide.

526
01:04:54,309 --> 01:05:04,097
Both of these microscopic approaches have low diagnostic sensitivity that approximates 60 percent (Krieger, 1988; Wiese, 2000).

527
01:05:04,097 --> 01:05:08,022
Pap tests can also yield false-positive results.

528
01:05:08,022 --> 01:05:14,459
Thus, Pap test trichomonad findings warrant confirmation with either a wet prep or NAAT.

529
01:05:14,459 --> 01:05:28,372
Of other direct detection methods, culture is expensive, lengthy, and only 75 to 95 percent sensitive (Association of Public Health Laboratories, 2016; Huppert, 2007).

530
01:05:28,372 --> 01:05:41,522
Laboratory NAAT-based evaluation of a vaginal, endocervical, or urine sample is available, is completed in minutes to hours, and offers superior sensitivity of 95 to 100 percent .

531
01:05:41,522 --> 01:05:51,522
Although more costly than wet-preparation microscopy, a NAAT may be a useful, second-line diagnostic test for microscopy-negative samples.

532
01:05:51,522 --> 01:05:55,572
An optimal approach in pregnancy has not been established.

533
01:05:55,572 --> 01:06:01,397
Rapid POC testing is also available but may sacrifice sensitivity for speed.

534
01:06:01,397 --> 01:06:11,409
The OSOM Trichomonas Rapid Test provides results in 10 minutes, is suitable for office use, and has sensitivities of 88 to 98 percent .

535
01:06:11,409 --> 01:06:15,009
Treatment Treatment is reserved for symptomatic women .

536
01:06:15,009 --> 01:06:22,197
Previously, a one-time, 2-g dose of oral metronidazole was used to eradicate T vaginalis.

537
01:06:22,197 --> 01:06:32,834
In nonpregnant women, a large randomized trial compared this traditional therapy against metronidazole, 500 mg orally twice daily for 7 days.

538
01:06:32,834 --> 01:06:36,284
The longer course provided improved efficacy .

539
01:06:36,284 --> 01:06:45,634
New guidelines from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020g) now recommend this change in nonpregnant women.

540
01:06:45,634 --> 01:06:49,597
The CDC recognizes this regimen for pregnancy.

541
01:06:49,597 --> 01:06:52,122
Table 68-8.

542
01:06:52,122 --> 01:07:00,047
Oral Treatment of Trichomoniasis in Pregnancya,b Reinfection rates among women treated for trichomoniasis are high .

543
01:07:00,047 --> 01:07:08,334
Thus, retesting for T vaginalis is recommended for all sexually active women within 3 months following initial therapy .

544
01:07:08,334 --> 01:07:13,459
Some authors recommend retesting 3 weeks after treatment in pregnancy .

545
01:07:13,459 --> 01:07:25,697
Metronidazole is not teratogenic or fetotoxic, and it may be used in all pregnancy stages (Briggs, 2022; Czeizel, 1998; Workowski, 2021).

546
01:07:25,697 --> 01:07:30,559
The manufacturer recommends against its use during the first trimester .

547
01:07:30,559 --> 01:07:36,034
Fewer data are available for tinidazole, and thus metronidazole is preferred.

548
01:07:36,034 --> 01:07:41,147
For allergic patients, metronidazole desensitization is effective .

549
01:07:41,147 --> 01:07:48,534
With breastfeeding, the longer, lower-dose metronidazole regimen is not associated with adverse infant outcomes .

550
01:07:48,534 --> 01:08:01,834
Perinatal transmission of trichomoniasis by direct contact in the birth canal is rare but may lead to neonatal respiratory or genital infection (Bruins, 2013; Trintis, 2010).

551
01:08:01,834 --> 01:08:06,297
Some studies have linked trichomonal infection with preterm birth .

552
01:08:06,297 --> 01:08:13,109
A few other studies implicate this infection with PPROM and small-for-gestational age newborns .

553
01:08:13,109 --> 01:08:22,497
However, treatment in two randomized studies did not lower preterm birth rates (Klebanoff, 2001; Stringer, 2010).

554
01:08:22,497 --> 01:08:26,359
In sum, treating symptomatic women is reasonable.

555
01:08:26,359 --> 01:08:31,284
For most asymptomatic women during pregnancy, screening is not done.

556
01:08:31,284 --> 01:08:39,347
However, screening at the first prenatal visit and prompt treatment are encouraged for gravidas with comorbid HIV infection.

557
01:08:39,347 --> 01:08:42,747
In this group, T vaginalis infection

558
01:08:42,916 --> 01:08:46,991
may be a risk factor for vertical HIV transmission .

559
01:08:46,941 --> 01:08:49,491
# Candidiasis.

560
01:08:49,491 --> 01:08:58,753
Candida albicans or other candidal species can be identified by culture from the vagina during pregnancy in approximately 20 percent of women.

561
01:08:58,753 --> 01:09:07,253
A link between candidiasis and preterm birth is not robust (Cotch, 1998; Roberts, 2015).

562
01:09:07,253 --> 01:09:11,653
Thus, asymptomatic colonization requires no treatment.

563
01:09:11,653 --> 01:09:16,791
The organism, however, can create a profuse, irritating discharge.

564
01:09:16,791 --> 01:09:21,891
For symptoms, effective treatment is listed in Table 68-9.

565
01:09:21,891 --> 01:09:29,178
In pregnancy, the CDC recommends 7-day regimens and suggests topical rather than oral azoles .

566
01:09:29,178 --> 01:09:34,466
In some women, infection is likely to recur and require repeat treatment.

567
01:09:34,466 --> 01:09:39,853
In these cases, symptomatic infection usually subsides after pregnancy .

568
01:09:39,853 --> 01:09:48,491
Although not preferred treatment, a single, 150-mg oral fluconazole dose is generally not considered teratogenic.

569
01:09:48,491 --> 01:09:59,828
Data showing a small association with cardiac anomalies are conflicting, but more recent evidence is reassuring (Mølgaard-Nielsen, 2013; Zhu, 2020).

570
01:09:59,828 --> 01:10:08,728
In 2019, the FDA noted that reviewed data do not provide conclusive evidence for an increased miscarriage or stillbirth risk.

571
01:10:08,728 --> 01:10:11,366
Table 68-9.

572
01:10:11,366 --> 01:10:20,641
Daily, Intravaginal Agents in 7-Day Regimens for Vulvovaginal Candidiasis Treatment in Pregnancy Human Immunodeficiency Virus.

573
01:10:20,641 --> 01:10:23,191
# Pathogenesis.

574
01:10:23,191 --> 01:10:32,066
For human immunodeficiency viruses, which are HIV-1 and HIV-2, sexual intercourse is the main mode of transmission.

575
01:10:32,066 --> 01:10:39,928
The virus can be passed by blood, and infected mothers may infect their fetuses during labor and delivery or by breast milk.

576
01:10:39,928 --> 01:10:45,041
The primary determinant of transmission is the maternal plasma viral load.

577
01:10:45,041 --> 01:10:51,441
For sexual transmission, the viral HIV envelope binds to mucosal dendritic cells.

578
01:10:51,441 --> 01:10:56,441
These cells then present the viral particle to specific T lymphocytes.

579
01:10:56,441 --> 01:11:05,216
These lymphocytes are defined phenotypically by their cluster of differentiation 4 (CD4) glycoprotein surface antigens.

580
01:11:05,216 --> 01:11:09,216
The CD4 site serves as a receptor for the virus.

581
01:11:09,216 --> 01:11:19,391
Once infected, CD4 T lymphocytes are gradually depleted, which creates an immunodeficiency that is characterized by low serum CD4 counts.

582
01:11:19,391 --> 01:11:31,266
In some infected with HIV, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) reflects profound immunodeficiency that gives rise to various opportunistic infections and neoplasms.

583
01:11:31,266 --> 01:11:37,078
The incubation period from exposure to clinical disease averages 3 to 6 weeks.

584
01:11:37,078 --> 01:11:44,241
Acute HIV infection is similar to many other viral syndromes and usually lasts less than 10 days.

585
01:11:44,241 --> 01:11:54,528
Common symptoms, if any, include fever, fatigue, rash, headache, lymphadenopathy, pharyngitis, myalgias, nausea, and diarrhea.

586
01:11:54,528 --> 01:12:05,241
After symptoms abate, the level of viremia usually declines to a set point, and patients with the highest viral burden at this time progress more rapidly to AIDS and death.

587
01:12:05,241 --> 01:12:20,603
According to the CDC, AIDS is defined by a CD4 T-cell count <200 cells/μL, by CD4 T cells constituting <14 percent of all lymphocytes, or by one of several AIDS-defining illnesses .

588
01:12:20,603 --> 01:12:29,716
Route of infection, viral strain’s pathogenicity, initial viral inoculum, and the host’s immunological status all affect progression.

589
01:12:29,716 --> 01:12:32,878
# Prenatal HIV Screening.

590
01:12:32,878 --> 01:12:38,953
In the United States, approximately 5000 women with HIV give birth annually .

591
01:12:38,953 --> 01:12:49,791
However, the estimated number of perinatally acquired HIV cases has dropped, and the rate was 0.9 per 100,000 live births in 2018 .

592
01:12:49,791 --> 01:12:58,628
The decline predominantly stems from prenatal HIV testing and antiretroviral therapy (ART) for the woman and then her neonate.

593
01:12:58,628 --> 01:13:10,666
The CDC (2006b) and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020f) recommend prenatal HIV screening using an opt-out approach.

594
01:13:10,666 --> 01:13:17,778
With this, HIV testing is routinely included in antenatal testing, but this testing may be declined.

595
01:13:17,778 --> 01:13:23,978
Women are given information regarding HIV but are not required to sign a specific consent.

596
01:13:23,978 --> 01:13:31,116
This opt-out strategy has raised HIV testing rates and is permitted in every United States jurisdiction .

597
01:13:31,116 --> 01:13:39,328
Repeat screening during the third trimester, preferably before 36 weeks’ gestation, is considered for all pregnant women.

598
01:13:39,328 --> 01:13:48,228
Retesting is recommended in areas where HIV rates exceed 1 case per 1000 pregnant women screened or for those with risk factors.

599
01:13:48,228 --> 01:13:50,041
These include

600
01:13:50,212 --> 01:14:02,212
incarceration, injection drug use, prostitution, a suspected or known HIV-infected sexual partner, a new or multiple sexual partners, or diagnosis of another STI .

601
01:14:02,162 --> 01:14:16,187
The initial laboratory screening test for HIV is an antigen/antibody combination immunoassay that detects antibodies against HIV-1 and HIV-2 and detects HIV-1 p24 antigen .

602
01:14:16,187 --> 01:14:25,649
No further testing is required for specimens that yield negative results on the initial immunoassay unless a known recent exposure to HIV has occurred.

603
01:14:25,649 --> 01:14:34,637
Antibody can be detected in most patients within 1 month of infection, and thus a test for antibodies alone may not exclude early infection.

604
01:14:34,637 --> 01:14:44,162
Instead, for acute primary HIV infection, identification of viral p24 core antigen or viral RNA is necessary.

605
01:14:44,162 --> 01:14:58,637
Specimens with a “reactive” (that is, a positive) antigen/antibody combination immunoassay result should be tested with an antibody immunoassay that differentiates HIV-1 antibodies from HIV-2 antibodies .

606
01:14:58,637 --> 01:15:14,762
The HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay provides results that are reported as positive or negative for HIV-1 antibodies, for HIV-2 antibodies, or for HIV antibodies, undifferentiated.

607
01:15:14,762 --> 01:15:28,074
If these two serial immunoassays (the initial screen and the differentiation assay) are discordant, an HIV-1 NAAT—qualitative or quantitative HIV RNA test—is performed .

608
01:15:28,074 --> 01:15:30,812
Figure 68-6.

609
01:15:30,812 --> 01:15:33,662
Algorithm for HIV testing.

610
01:15:33,662 --> 01:15:51,399
For specimens that are reactive on the initial antigen/antibody combination immunoassay and nonreactive or indeterminate on the HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay, a nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is performed.

611
01:15:51,399 --> 01:16:04,937
Following a nonreactive HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay, a positive HIV-1 NAAT indicates laboratory evidence for acute HIV-1 infection.

612
01:16:04,937 --> 01:16:19,887
A positive HIV-1 NAAT result following indeterminate HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay indicates the presence of HIV-1 infection confirmed by HIV-1 NAAT.

613
01:16:19,887 --> 01:16:41,449
A negative HIV-1 NAAT result following nonreactive or indeterminate HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay indicates a false-positive result on the initial antigen/antibody combination immunoassay. (Reproduced with permission from Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 2018a.

614
01:16:41,449 --> 01:16:52,437
At delivery, women with undocumented HIV status should have a fourthgeneration HIV antigen/antibody combination screening test performed on a blood sample.

615
01:16:52,437 --> 01:16:56,487
A negative screening test result does not need confirmation.

616
01:16:56,487 --> 01:17:07,624
However, in cases of recent HIV exposure, consideration is given to peripartum interventions to reduce perinatal transmission despite negative HIV testing.

617
01:17:07,624 --> 01:17:14,612
Repeat interval testing is recommended to exclude very early infection not identified with the initial screen.

618
01:17:14,612 --> 01:17:24,199
With a positive fourthgeneration HIV testing result, peripartum and neonatal interventions to reduce perinatal transmission are initiated.

619
01:17:24,199 --> 01:17:34,499
These include intravenous zidovudine infusion during labor and avoidance of breastfeeding, although breast milk may be stored until confirmatory test results are available .

620
01:17:34,499 --> 01:17:44,399
Interventions are continued until the results of an antibody differentiation assay—and qualitative or quantitative RNA test if indicated—is known.

621
01:17:44,399 --> 01:17:49,262
Interventions can be discontinued if confirmatory testing is negative.

622
01:17:49,262 --> 01:18:01,924
To confirm a positive result from any initial HIV test, the laboratory testing algorithm in Figure 68-6 is used and begins with the antigen/antibody combination immunoassay.

623
01:18:01,924 --> 01:18:04,662
# Vertical Transmission.

624
01:18:04,662 --> 01:18:09,299
Viral burden and neonatal infection rates are directly related.

625
01:18:09,299 --> 01:18:22,324
In one cohort, neonatal infection was 1 percent with <400 copies/mL, and it was 23 percent when maternal viral RNA levels were >30,000 copies/mL .

626
01:18:22,324 --> 01:18:35,624
Among 2615 neonates born to mothers taking ART before conception and during pregnancy, no cases of vertical transmission occurred when maternal viral loads were <50 copies/mL at delivery .

627
01:18:35,624 --> 01:18:45,874
Transmission of HIV infection, however, has been observed at all HIV RNA levels, including those that were nondetectable by current assays.

628
01:18:45,874 --> 01:18:53,849
Transplacental HIV transmission can occur early, and the virus has even been identified in specimens from elective

629
01:18:54,004 --> 01:18:55,616
abortion .

630
01:18:55,566 --> 01:19:08,466
Kourtis and colleagues (2001) estimated that 20 percent of vertical transmission occurs before 36 weeks’ gestation, 50 percent in the days before delivery, and 30 percent intrapartum.

631
01:19:08,466 --> 01:19:21,729
Transmission rates for breastfeeding may be as high as 30 to 40 percent and are associated with increasing HIV viral burden (Kourtis, 2006, 2007; Slyker, 2012).

632
01:19:21,729 --> 01:19:28,666
In nonpregnant individuals, concomitant STIs and horizontal HIV transmission are linked.

633
01:19:28,666 --> 01:19:38,316
Evidence also supports that vertical transmission rates may be increased by comorbid STIs (Schulte, 2001; Watts, 2012).

634
01:19:38,316 --> 01:19:40,941
# Antepartum Care.

635
01:19:40,941 --> 01:19:49,229
Pregnant women with HIV infection need special attention and are seen in consultation by physicians with special interest in this field.

636
01:19:49,229 --> 01:20:03,404
An additional resource is the National Perinatal HIV Hotline (1–888–448–8765), which provides free antepartum, intrapartum, or postpartum consultation to providers.

637
01:20:03,404 --> 01:20:36,779
At Parkland Hospital, a pregnant woman with HIV infection is initially assessed with the following: • Standard prenatal laboratory surveys that include serum creatinine, complete blood count, and bacteriuria screening • Plasma HIV RNA quantification, which establishes the “viral load” value, and antiretroviral resistance testing if the viral load is at least 500 to 1000 copies/mL • CD4 T-cell count • Screening for chlamydial infection, gonorrhea, syphilis, and trichomoniasis, if not previously

638
01:20:36,779 --> 01:21:10,416
performed with new HIV diagnosis • Serum hepatic aminotransferase levels • HSV-1 and -2, cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis, and hepatitis B and C serological screening • Baseline chest radiograph • Tuberculosis testing with purified protein derivative (PPD) skin testing or with interferon-gamma release assay • Evaluation of need for pneumococcal, hepatitis A, hepatitis B, Tdap, COVID-19, and influenza vaccines • Sonographic evaluation to establish gestational age.

639
01:21:10,416 --> 01:21:23,204
During pregnancy, the risk of HIV transmission does not appear to be increased with amniocentesis or other invasive diagnostic procedures in women receiving effective ART that results in viral suppression .

640
01:21:23,204 --> 01:21:28,216
For women not receiving ART, the risk rises approximately twofold .

641
01:21:28,216 --> 01:21:34,029
If amniocentesis is performed, efforts are taken to avoid passing through the placenta .

642
01:21:34,029 --> 01:21:55,354
Antiretroviral Therapy In overview, the ideal strategy to suppress viral load and minimize vertical HIV transmission includes (1) preconceptional ART, (2) antepartum ART, (3) intrapartum continuation of the antepartum oral ART regimen plus IV zidovudine, and (4) newborn ART prophylaxis.

643
01:21:55,354 --> 01:22:03,316
ART is recommended for all pregnant women with HIV infection, and it should be initiated as early in pregnancy as possible.

644
01:22:03,316 --> 01:22:10,929
Treatment reduces the risk of perinatal transmission regardless of CD4 T-cell count or HIV RNA level.

645
01:22:10,929 --> 01:22:15,904
Regimen adherence is emphasized to help prevent viral drug resistance.

646
01:22:15,904 --> 01:22:21,341
Gravidas are treated with at least three antiretroviral (ARV) agents.

647
01:22:21,341 --> 01:22:33,154
The Panel on Treatment of Pregnant Women with HIV Infection and Prevention of Perinatal Transmission (2021) has issued guidelines for four different scenarios during pregnancy .

648
01:22:33,154 --> 01:22:37,129
The following paragraphs summarize these recommendations.

649
01:22:37,129 --> 01:22:39,654
Table 68-10.

650
01:22:39,654 --> 01:22:53,529
Recommendations for HIV Antiviral Drug Use During Pregnancy First, women already taking ART at pregnancy onset are encouraged to continue their regimen if viral suppression is adequate and if the ARV is safe and tolerated.

651
01:22:53,529 --> 01:23:04,404
Didanosine, stavudine, and full-dose ritonavir, which differs from ritonavir-boosted agents, are exceptions due to pregnancy toxicity but not teratogenicity.

652
01:23:04,404 --> 01:23:13,104
The lopinavir/ritonavir combination is no longer recommended based on risks for preterm and small-for-gestational-age neonates.

653
01:23:13,104 --> 01:23:21,779
Second, women who have never received antiretroviral therapy— antiretroviral naïve—are given ART regardless of trimester.

654
01:23:21,779 --> 01:23:34,329
In general, the starting regimen comprises two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors that are coupled into a single agent plus either a ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor or an integrase inhibitor.

655
01:23:34,329 --> 01:23:40,691
Third, women who have previously received antiretroviral therapy but are currently not taking

656
01:23:40,852 --> 01:23:48,127
medications should undergo HIV-resistance testing because prior ART use raises the risk of drug resistance.

657
01:23:48,077 --> 01:23:53,914
However, ART is initiated prior to receiving results of these drug-resistance tests.

658
01:23:53,914 --> 01:24:06,802
In this case, initial ART selection should factor results of any prior resistance testing, if available; prior ART regimen; and current ART pregnancy guidelines, that is, those for ART-naïve women.

659
01:24:06,802 --> 01:24:10,989
Drug-resistance testing may then modify the initial regimen.

660
01:24:10,989 --> 01:24:19,077
For these three categories of women taking antepartum ART, therapy surveillance is outlined in Table 68-10.

661
01:24:19,077 --> 01:24:27,364
Most patients with adequate viral response have at least a 1-log viral load decline within 1 to 4 weeks after starting therapy.

662
01:24:27,364 --> 01:24:34,752
Newer integrase inhibitors rapidly decrease viral loads and are useful for women presenting late to prenatal care .

663
01:24:34,752 --> 01:24:44,302
For those who fail to achieve this degree of decline, regimen compliance is assessed, resistance studies are reviewed, and ART modification is considered.

664
01:24:44,302 --> 01:24:49,914
During labor and delivery, an oral ART regimen is continued with sips of water.

665
01:24:49,914 --> 01:24:56,264
Additionally, IV zidovudine is administered to women during labor and before cesarean delivery.

666
01:24:56,264 --> 01:25:13,689
An exception to this addition is the patient who is strictly adherent to her prescribed oral ART regimen and who has an HIV RNA level that was previously documented to be <50 copies/mL at 34 to 36 weeks’ gestation or at 3 to 4 weeks before delivery.

667
01:25:13,689 --> 01:25:22,164
At Parkland Hospital, we administer intrapartum IV zidovudine to all HIV-positive women, regardless of viral load.

668
01:25:22,164 --> 01:25:31,652
A 2-mg/kg loading dose is infused over 1 hour and is followed with zidovudine, 1 mg/kg/hr until delivery.

669
01:25:31,652 --> 01:25:45,902
Women with HIV undergoing a scheduled cesarean delivery are given IV zidovudine as a loading dose followed by 2 more hours of continuous maintenance therapy— a total of 3 hours of infused zidovudine prior to surgery.

670
01:25:45,902 --> 01:25:51,239
The fourth group includes women who present in labor and who are taking no ART regimen.

671
01:25:51,239 --> 01:25:55,989
These women are given IV zidovudine intrapartum as just described.

672
01:25:55,989 --> 01:25:58,477
# Delivery Planning.

673
01:25:58,477 --> 01:26:04,439
In some cases, cesarean delivery lowers HIV prenatal transmission rates .

674
01:26:04,439 --> 01:26:20,102
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) suggests that scheduled cesarean delivery be discussed and recommended for HIV-infected women with HIV-1 RNA load >1000 copies/mL.

675
01:26:20,102 --> 01:26:26,914
Scheduled delivery is recommended at 38 weeks’ gestation in these women to avoid spontaneous labor.

676
01:26:26,914 --> 01:26:38,639
Management of women with either HIV-1 RNA load >1000 copies/mL or unknown viral load who present in labor or with ruptured membranes is individualized.

677
01:26:38,639 --> 01:26:43,864
For these women, IV zidovudine is recommended as previously described.

678
01:26:43,864 --> 01:26:49,289
In the case of early labor, cesarean delivery is preferred to vaginal birth.

679
01:26:49,289 --> 01:27:10,989
For women with HIV RNA load ≤1000 copies/mL, cesarean delivery to reduce the risk of vertical HIV transmission is not recommended and is unlikely to confer additional benefit to women already taking ART and achieving viral suppression (Briand, 2013; Jamieson, 2007).

680
01:27:10,989 --> 01:27:17,264
Vaginal delivery in this group is the goal, and awaiting spontaneous onset of labor is suitable.

681
01:27:17,264 --> 01:27:22,002
Labor is not induced to prevent perinatal HIV transmission.

682
01:27:22,002 --> 01:27:37,214
During labor, artificial membrane rupture, fetal scalp electrode placement, episiotomy, and operative vaginal delivery are performed for standard obstetrical indications but are avoided if possible in women with detectable RNA viral load .

683
01:27:37,214 --> 01:27:44,264
Labor augmentation is used when needed to shorten the interval to delivery to help further lower the transmission risk.

684
01:27:44,264 --> 01:27:54,777
For the benefits described in Chapter 27 (p. 500), delayed cord clamping in preterm neonates is acceptable and may be considered for term newborns.

685
01:27:54,777 --> 01:27:57,827
Neuraxial analgesia is suitable.

686
01:27:57,827 --> 01:28:03,427
Postpartum hemorrhage is best managed with oxytocin and prostaglandin analogues.

687
01:28:03,427 --> 01:28:13,927
Methylergonovine (Methergine) and other ergot alkaloids adversely interact with reverse transcriptase and protease inhibitors to cause severe vasoconstriction.

688
01:28:13,927 --> 01:28:16,477
# Postpartum Care.

689
01:28:16,477 --> 01:28:27,364
Vertical transmission rates rise with breastfeeding, and it generally is not recommended for women with HIV infection in the United States, where formula is readily available .

690
01:28:27,364 --> 01:28:28,702
In

691
01:28:28,852 --> 01:28:43,014
nutritionally deprived countries, where infectious disease and malnutrition are primary causes of infant death, the World Health Organization (2016) recommends exclusive breastfeeding during the first 6 to 12 months.

692
01:28:42,964 --> 01:28:58,252
The Panel on Treatment of Pregnant Women with HIV Infection and Prevention of Perinatal Transmission (2021) strongly recommends that ART regimens not be discontinued postpartum but continued lifelong for the advantages of viral suppression.

693
01:28:58,252 --> 01:29:05,989
Ideally, all those planning pregnancy should be receiving ART and have a viral load below detectable levels before conception.

694
01:29:05,989 --> 01:29:18,189
As one benefit, interpregnancy viral load suppression is associated with less vertical transmission in a subsequent pregnancy (French, 2014; Stewart, 2014).

695
01:29:18,189 --> 01:29:29,264
Reassuringly, for those seeking subsequent pregnancy, when ART is available, repeated pregnancy in a healthy woman with HIV has no significant effect on disease progression .

696
01:29:29,264 --> 01:29:35,177
Transition postpartum to general HIV care is critical to maintain viral suppression .

697
01:29:35,177 --> 01:29:37,327
# Prevention.

698
01:29:37,327 --> 01:29:51,789
For adults without HIV infection but with risks for HIV acquisition, the USPSTF (2019b) recommends offering preexposure prophylaxis (PrEP) to decrease the risk of acquiring HIV.

699
01:29:51,789 --> 01:30:04,889
Risk factors for heterosexually active women without HIV infection are a partner with HIV, syphilis or gonorrhea within the past 6 months, and inconsistent condom use with a high-risk partner.

700
01:30:04,889 --> 01:30:10,477
The last is a partner with injection drug use or with multiple other sexual partners.

701
01:30:10,477 --> 01:30:23,177
Current guidance from the CDC (2018b) emphasizes the use of ART to achieve viral suppression in the infected partner and barrier use as primary methods of preventing horizontal transmission.

702
01:30:23,177 --> 01:30:33,527
However, PrEP for the HIV-negative partner is considered, including during pregnancy to reduce the risk of HIV acquisition and also vertical transmission.

703
01:30:33,527 --> 01:30:48,252
PrEP with tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/emtricitabine taken orally daily is acceptable during pregnancy and is encouraged particularly if the HIV viral load of the infected partner is unknown or unsuppressed.

704
01:30:48,252 --> 01:30:51,002
Barrier methods are reinforced.

705
01:30:51,002 --> 01:30:57,877
For conception in HIV-serodiscordant couples, assisted reproductive technologies are available.

706
01:30:57,877 --> 01:31:03,464
In lower-resource settings, acceptable approaches combine safer conception methods.

707
01:31:03,464 --> 01:31:25,027
These include combinations of treatment to achieve viral suppression for the HIV-seropositive partner, PrEP for the HIV-seronegative partner, STI screening and treatment, timed condomless sex during peak fertility days, and self-insemination, (American Society for Reproductive Medicine, 2021; Schwartz, 2019).

708
01:31:25,027 --> 01:31:29,852
If pregnancy is undesired, contraception options are discussed .

709
01:31:29,852 --> 01:31:40,389
Counseling also includes education for decreasing high-risk sexual behaviors to prevent HIV transmission or acquisition and to decrease other STI acquisition.

